      <!-- SECTION 2 -->
      <section class="section" id="cleaning-chemistry">
        <h2>The Chemistry of Effective Cleaning: Active Ingredients</h2>
        <p>
          Professional-grade toilet bowl cleaners generally belong to the acidic category, as they must tackle mineral deposits (limescale), rust, and uric acid stains. Understanding the molecular interactions between the cleaner and the ceramic surface is the first step toward creating a formulation that outperforms mass-market competitors. The chemical equilibrium at the surface interface determines the product's ability to chelate metal ions and break down complex organic matrices.
        </p>
        <p>
          The active cleaning agents must be carefully selected to ensure high efficacy without being overly aggressive on the drainage infrastructure. This technical selection process differentiates a standard product from a premium industrial-grade cleaner, where sustainability and material compatibility are prioritized alongside aggressive stain removal capabilities.
        </p>
        
        <h3 style="color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.4rem; margin-top: 24px; margin-bottom: 12px; font-family: 'Playfair Display', serif;">1. Active Acids: The Cleaning Powerhouse</h3>
        <p>
          The choice of acid defines the speed and efficiency of the stain removal process. While Hydrochloric Acid remains the industrial standard for fast-acting performance due to its high dissociation constant, modern formulations are increasingly incorporating organic acids such as Citric, Sulfamic, or Lactic acid for "green" variants. These organic alternatives offer lower toxicity profiles while maintaining significant descaling power.
        </p>
        <p>
          These acids work by dissolving the mineral matrix of the stain, allowing it to be easily rinsed away. Technical accuracy in selecting the specific acid concentration—typically ranging from 8% to 15% in industrial formulations—is vital to ensure consistent performance across various water hardness levels without compromising the integrity of ceramic glazes or plumbing seals.
        </p>

        <h3 style="color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.4rem; margin-top: 24px; margin-bottom: 12px; font-family: 'Playfair Display', serif;">2. Surfactants: Wetting and Soil Removal</h3>
        <p>
          Surfactants play a dual role in toilet cleaner formulations: they reduce surface tension to allow the acid to penetrate deep into stains and help emulsify oily residues. In highly acidic environments, non-ionic surfactants like Alcohol Ethoxylates (e.g., C12-15 Ethoxylates) are preferred due to their stability and high wetting power. Anionic surfactants are often avoided in acidic gels as they can lead to instability or precipitate formation.
        </p>
        <p>
          Selecting the right surfactant also impacts the foam profile of the product. Industrial consultants often recommend specific blends to ensure that the cleaner provides enough foam to signify "cleaning action" to the user while maintaining easy rinsability. A low-foaming profile is often preferred for industrial applications where speed of flushing is critical for turnaround times.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- SECTION 3 -->
      <section class="section" id="thickening-rheology">
        <h2>Thickening &amp; Rheology: The Science of the "Cling"</h2>
        <p>
          One of the most critical aspects of a professional-grade cleaner is its viscosity and yield value. A low-viscosity liquid will simply run down the toilet bowl sides without providing enough contact time to dissolve stains. High-performance cleaners utilize advanced thickening systems to ensure "vertical cling," allowing the chemical action to take place over 5 to 10 minutes of contact. This contact time is essential for the acid to penetrate the crystalline structure of calcium carbonate deposits.
        </p>
        <p>
          The science of rheology in cleaning products involves choosing thickeners that remain stable in low pH environments. This is a common failure point for many startup formulations, leading to product separation or loss of thickness over time. Achieving a "pseudoplastic" flow behavior is the gold standard, where the product flows easily when squeezed through a nozzle but thickens immediately upon contact with the surface, preventing wasteful runoff:
        </p>
        <ul style="padding-left: 20px; margin-bottom: 24px;">
          <li><strong>Acid-Stable Gums</strong>: Natural polymers like Xanthan Gum are often used for their excellent shear-thinning (pseudoplastic) properties. They maintain viscosity even in the presence of high acid concentrations and provide a smooth, consistent pour.</li>
          <li><strong>Amine Oxides</strong>: These specialized surfactants (e.g., Lauryl Amine Oxide) function as both cleaning agents and secondary thickeners in acidic solutions. They provide a clear, gel-like appearance that consumers associate with premium quality.</li>
          <li><strong>Associative Thickeners</strong>: Synthetic polymers can provide a highly transparent, clear gel appearance and can be engineered to offer specific flow characteristics tailored for automated bottling lines.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <!-- Image 3 -->
      <div class="image-slot">
        <img 
          src="https://assets.zyrosite.com/AGBzPMBJDQiwDGny/quality_control_bottling-0oz33O251MK57X18.png" 
